CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to rationally develop and use water resources and to promote the development of the national economy by providing for matters regarding the construction and management of dams, the revolving investment in the costs of dam construction, environmental measures following the construction of dams, and support for residents in the areas adjacent to dams.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The term "dam" means a structure rising at least 15 meter high, built, by blocking the flow of a river, to be used as tap water, water for industrial use, water for agriculture, water for environmental reform industrial water, agricultural water, environmental water, power generation, flood control, transportation by ship, and other purposes (hereinafter referred to as "specific purposes"), including spillways, auxiliary dams, and other facilities or structures integrated into the relevant dam to maximize the utility of the dam;
  2. The term "multipurpose dam" means a dam constructed by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for at least 2 specific purposes (excluding facilities or structures to be used exclusively for a specific purpose);
  3. The term "right to use a dam" means the right to secure a certain quantity of water reserved in a multipurpose dam in a certain area and use the water for a specific purpose;
  4. The term "dam management" means any and all activities for a general maintenance and management of a dam subject to this Act;
  5. The term "residents relocated from an area to be submerged" means persons who are to lose the basis of their livelihood due to the implementation of a dam construction project and thus become subject to measures for relocation, as provided for in Article 78(1) of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 3 (Scope of application) (1) This Act shall apply to multipurpose dams and dams constructed by any of the following persons, in order to supply tap water, water for industrial use, or water for environmental reform or to control floods: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment;
  2. The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor");
  3. The head of a Si/Gun;
  4. Other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), Article 4 and 4-2 shall apply to the following dams: <Added on Jun. 15, 2021>
  1. A dam built by a person falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1);
  2. A power generation dam that has gone through deliberation by the Executive Committee for Electric Power Source Development Project under Article 4 of the Electric Power Source Development Promotion Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

CHAPTER II CONSTRUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OF DAMS

SECTION 1 Management of Dams

Article 4 (Master plan for dam management) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall formulate a master plan for dam management every 10 years (hereinafter referred to as "master plan for dam management"), including the following matters, in order to manage dams and conserve their environs: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Basic policies of dam management; r
  2. Plans for managing dam facilities;
  3. Plans for operating dam reservoir and conserving the water environment (referring to the water environment defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Water Environment Conservation Act);
  4. Schemes to conserve the environs of dams (limited to dams falling under Article 3(2)1);
  5. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree to efficiently manage dams.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall review the feasibility of a master plan for dam management every 5 years from the date it is formulated, and modify the plan, if necessary. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to formulate or modify a master plan for dam management in accordance with paragraphs (1) and (2), he or she shall require the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to formulate or modify master plans regarding dam management for their respective jurisdictions and shall reflect such plans in the relevant master plan for dam management. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the head of a relevant central administrative agency and a person who manages a dam under the subparagraphs of Article 3(2) (including a person entrusted with the management of the relevant dam) to submit data necessary for formulating or modifying a master plan for dam management. In such cases, a person in receipt of such request shall comply therewith, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to formulate or modify a master plan for dam management in accordance with paragraphs (1) and (2), he or she shall hear the opinion of the competent Mayor/Do Governor, consult with the head of a relevant central administrative agency, and then undergo deliberation by the National Water Resources Management Committee under Article 29 of the Act on the Investigation, Planning, and Management of Water Resources. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Upon formulation or modification of a master plan for dam management in accordance with paragraphs (1) and (2), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall submit the plan to the National Assembly and notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and competent Mayors/Do Governors of such plan without delay. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (7) Matters necessary for formulation, modification, etc. of a master plan for dam management under paragraphs (1) through (6) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [Previous Article 4 moved to Article 10 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 4-2 (Detailed implementation plan for dam management) (1) A person who manages a dam under the subparagraphs of Article 3(2) shall formulate a detailed implementation plan for dam management for a dam under his or her jurisdiction (hereinafter referred to as "detailed implementation plan") in accordance with the relevant master plan for dam management.
  (2) If a person who manages a dam under Article 3(2)1 intends to formulate a detailed implementation plan in accordance with paragraph (1) or to modify a detailed implementation plan formulated, he or she shall obtain, in advance, approval therefor from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment; provided, this shall not apply to any modification to minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) If a person who manages a dam under Article 3(2)2 intends to formulate a detailed implementation plan in accordance with paragraph (1) or to modify a detailed implementation plan formulated, he or she shall have a prior consultation thereon with the head of the competent central administrative agency and notify the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment thereof; provided, this shall not apply to any modification to minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) If a detailed implementation plan is formulated or modified under paragraphs (1) through (3), the details thereof shall be disclosed to the public.
  (5) The procedures and methods for formulating and modifying detailed implementation plans under paragraphs (1) through (3) and matters necessary for disclosing detailed implementation plans under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 15, 2021]

Article 5 (Administrative authorities responsible for dam management and managers entrusted with dam management) (1) Dams shall be managed by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment; provided, the dams constructed by a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun shall be managed by the competent Mayor/Do Governor or the head of the competent Si/Gun. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun, who is responsible for the management of a dam in accordance with paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "dam management agency") may entrust the management of the dam to a person who holds the right to use the dam (hereinafter referred to "dam usage right-holder") or a public institution under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, which installs and operates dams as he or she may deem necessary for the management of the dam. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Matters necessary for the entrustment of dam management under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 15 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 6 (Basic principles of dam management) Dams shall be managed properly to prevent flood and drought, to enhance public interests with water stored in the dams, to eliminate or mitigate injuries and losses, and to avoid the violation of a right to use a dam. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 16 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 7 (Dam management regulations) (1) A dam management agency shall establish dam management regulations in compliance with the basic principles of dam management under Article 6, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  (2) A person entrusted with the management of a dam under Article 5(2) (hereinafter referred to as "dam custodian") shall prepare dam management regulations and obtain approval thereof from the competent dam management agency. The same shall also apply where he or she intends to amend the dam management regulations. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  (3) Where a dam management agency intends to establish or approve dam management regulations pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), it shall have a prior consultation thereon with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and shall hear the opinions of the grantee of dam usage rights or the dam usage right-holder. The same shall also apply where it is intended to amend the dam management regulations.
  (4) If deemed necessary for the efficient management of water resources, a dam management agency may order the dam custodian to amend the relevant dam management regulations under paragraph (2), and require the person to submit a report on the management of the dam, or give instructions to the person, as necessary.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 17; previous Article 7 moved to Article 11 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 8 (Measures to prevent hazards) If a dam management agency or a dam custodian finds that a discharge of water stored in the relevant dam is likely to result in a significant change in the downstream, the agency or person shall notify the relevant Mayors/Do Governors of the intended discharge to prevent risks, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and shall take measures to inform the general public thereof.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 18; previous Article 8 moved to Article 12 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 9 (Projects for dam management) If necessary to manage a dam appropriately, the competent dam management agency or the dam custodian may implement the following projects, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020; Jun. 15, 2021>
  1. Projects for eliminating or alleviating flood damage, traffic inconvenience, etc., caused by the dam;
  2. Projects for enhancing the utility of the dam, such as projects for creating rest areas and installing sports facilities;
  3. Projects for managing the water environment in an area upstream of a dam to improve the quality of water in the dam.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 18-2; previous Article 9 moved to Article 13 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 9-2 (Evaluation of dams) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall require a dam management agency to evaluate the dam spillway capacity, flood control capacity, etc. on a regular basis in order to better utilize the dam and efficiently manage water resources, and may utilize the results of the evaluation in the formulation of a plan related to water resources, such as a master plan for national water management under Article 27 of the Framework Act on Water Management. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Evaluation under paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2016]
  [Moved from Article 18-3; previous Article 9-2 moved to Article 13-2 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 10 (Examination of appropriateness of dam construction) (1) If a person falling under any subparagraph of Article 3(1) intends to construct a dam, he or she shall formulate a plan for constructing the dam, including the following matters, and a person falling under subparagraphs 2 through 4 of that paragraph shall submit such plan to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment: <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The project name;
  2. The objectives and necessity of the project;
  3. The location and location map of the candidate site for the dam;
  4. The scale and type of the dam;
  5. The site to be submerged, the area of the site to be submerged, and the number of households submerged;
  6. The approximate project costs;
  7. The effects of the project;
  8. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall examine the appropriateness of the plan for constructing a dam formulated and submitted in accordance with paragraph (1), gather opinions from local residents deemed to have interests in constructing the dam, such as that the dam is to be constructed in the relevant area, and reflect such opinions in a river basin water resource management plan under Article 18 of the Act on the Investigation, Planning, and Management of Water Resources (hereinafter referred to as "river basin water resource management plan"). <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall disclose the results of gathering opinions from local residents and of reflecting such opinions in a river basin water resource management plan under paragraph (2). <Added on Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may establish a preliminary examination council that examines the following in order to examine the appropriateness of a plan for dam construction in accordance with paragraph (2): <Added on Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The objectives and necessity of a dam project;
  2. An alternative to the construction of a dam and the feasibility thereof;
  3. The possibility of accepting a dam project by local residents;
  4. Other matters deemed necessary to determine whether to implement a dam project.
  (5) Matters necessary for the methods and procedures for examining the appropriateness under paragraph (2), the methods for disclosing the results under paragraph (3), and the composition, operation, etc. of the preliminary examination council under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Title Amended on Jun. 15, 2021]
  [Moved from Article 4; previous Article 10 moved to Article 14 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 11 (Master plans) (1) A person who intends to construct a dam (hereinafter referred to as "implementer of a dam construction project") shall formulate a master plan for constructing a dam (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan"), which shall include the following matters; provided, the matters specified in subparagraph 12 shall be included only where a dam meets or exceeds the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree pursuant to Article 41(1): <Amended on Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 15, 2021; Jan. 30, 2024>
  1. Objectives of construction;
  2. The name of the project and the location and surface area of the project site;
  3. Size and type;
  4. The water storage capacity and the distribution of stored water by purpose;
  5. The name or title of the person to whom the right to use the dam is to be granted (hereinafter referred to as "grantee of dam usage rights") and the contents of the rights thereto;
  6. The costs of dam construction (including the cost to be borne by the implementer of the dam construction project on the improvement of the areas adjacent to the dam under Article 42) and matters regarding the allocation of the cost;
  7. The implementer of a dam construction project;
  8. The project period;
  9. The detailed list of the land, buildings, and other articles or rights to be expropriated or used;
  10. Anticipated damage to the environment as a result of dam construction, a scheme for mitigating such damage, and the preservation of the quality of water in the dam;
  11. Projects that can contribute to social, cultural and economic development of the areas adjacent to the dam and enhancement of the effectiveness of the dam;
  12. Location and Area of an area deemed designated as a regional development project zone under the Regional Development Assistance Act (hereinafter referred to as "regional development project zone") pursuant to Article 13(1) (limited to the scope of dam environs under Article 41(4));
  13. Other basic matters regarding the construction of the dam.
  (2) If the implementer of a dam construction project, who is neither the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment nor a Mayor/Do Governor, intends to formulate a master plan in accordance with paragraph (1), he or she shall obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (referring to the competent Mayor/Do Governor, if the head of a Si/Gun intends to formulate a master plan). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to formulate or approve a master plan in accordance with paragraph (1) or (2), he or she shall, in advance, consult with the heads of relevant administrative agencies and hear the opinions of the relevant grantee of dam usage rights. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor formulates or approves a master plan, he or she shall publicly notify the plan, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) The provisions of paragraphs (1) through (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis where he or she intends to modify or repeal a master plan (excluding modification of minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 7; previous Article 11 moved to Article 15 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 12 (Implementation plans) (1) The implementer of a dam construction project shall formulate an implementation plan for constructing a dam (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plan") after formulating a relevant master plan, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the implementer of a dam construction project, who is neither the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment nor a Mayor/Do Governor, intends to formulate an implementation plan, he or she shall obtain approval, in advance, from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (referring to the competent Mayor/Do Governor, if the head of a Si/Gun intends to formulate an implementation plan). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor formulates or approves an implementation plan in accordance with paragraph (1) or (2), he or she shall, without delay, publicly notify the details thereof and notify the heads of relevant administrative agencies and the grantee of dam usage rights thereof. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Paragraphs (1) through (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis where he or she intends to modify or repeal an implementation plan (excluding modification of minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 8; previous Article 12 moved to Article 16 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 13 (Legal fiction of authorization and permission under other Acts) (1) Where the Minister of Environment or the Mayor/Do Governor has consulted with the heads of relevant administrative agencies in advance when he or she formulates, modified, or approves a master plan in accordance with Article 11(1), (2), and (5), the area under Article 11(1)12 shall be deemed designated as a regional development project zone under Article 11 of the Regional Development Assistance Act; and where such master plan is publicly notified in accordance with Article 11(4), the designation of a regional development project zone shall be deemed publicly notified. In such cases, the Regional Development Assistance Act apply to regional development project zones, except as otherwise provided in this Act. <Amended on Jan. 30, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Mayor/Do Governor formulates, modifies, or approves an implementation plan in accordance with Article 12(1), (2), or (4), the permission, reporting, cancellation, consultation, approval, authorization, designation, etc. referred to in the following subparagraphs (hereinafter referred to as "authorization, permission, etc.") shall be deemed granted, made, or obtained; when such implementation plan is publicly notified in accordance with Article 12(3), the public notice or public announcement of authorization, permission, etc. referred to in the following subparagraphs shall be deemed given or made: <Amended on May 30, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 14, 2014; Dec. 2, 2016; Dec. 27, 2016; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Dec. 27, 2022; Jan. 30, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The permission to occupy and use public waters under Article 8 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act; the license to reclaim public waters under Article 28 of that Act; or the approval of an implementation plan to reclaim public waters under Article 38 of that Act;
  2. The determination of an urban management plan under Article 30 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act (only limited to facilities defined in subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act); the permission to engage in development activities under Article 56 of that Act; the designation of the implementer of an urban planning facility project under Article 86 of that Act; or the authorization for an implementation plan under Article 88 of that Act;
  3. The consultation on the permission, etc. by an administrative agency under Article 13 of the Protection of Military Bases and Installations Act;
  3-2. The permission to maintain and improve a road under Article 5 of the Act on the Maintenance and Improvement of Road Networks in Agricultural and Fishing Villages, and permission to occupy and use a road under Article 18 of that Act;
  4. The permission to use infrastructure for agricultural production under Article 23 of the Rearrangement of Agricultural and Fishing Villages Act;
  5. The permission to consult on, divert farmland under Article 34 of the Farmland Act;
  6. The permission to implement road works under Article 36 of the Road Act or the permission to occupy and use a road under Article 61 of that Act;
  7. The permission to open a private road under Article 4 of the Private Road Act;
  8. The permission to cut trees, etc. under Article 14 of the Erosion Control Work Act or cancellation of the designation of an erosion control area under Article 20 of that Act;
  9. The permission for, or the reporting of, the diversion of a mountainous district under Article 14 or 15 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act; the permission for, or the reporting of, the temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 15-2 of that Act; the permission for gathering rocks and soil (limited to building stones) under Article 25 of that Act; the permission for, or the reporting of, cutting standing timber under Article 36(1) or (5) of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act; or the permission for, or the reporting of, activities in a reserved forest (excluding a conservation zone for forest gene resources under Article 9(1) and (2)1 or 2 of the Forest Protection Act;
  10. The permission to occupy and use under Article 14 of the Small River Maintenance Act;
  11. The permission to engage in acts under Article 23 of the Natural Parks Act;
  12. The permission to relocate graves under Article 27(1) of the Act on Funeral Services, etc.;
  13. The permission to develop grassland under Article 5 of the Grassland Act or the permission to divert grassland under Article 23 of that Act;
  14. The approval for, or the reporting of, the installation of a waste disposal facility under Article 29 of the Wastes Control Act;
  15. The permission to implement a public sewerage system (limited to facilities for the treatment of excreta) under Article 11 of the Sewerage Act; the permission to implement a public sewerage project under Article 16 of that Act; or the permission to occupy and use under Article 24 of that Act;
  16. The consultation on, or the approval for, matters subject to permission by the competent river management authorities under Article 6 of the River Act; the permission to implement a river project under Article 30 of that Act; the permission to occupy and use a river under Article 33 of that Act; or the permission to use water from a river under Article 50 of that Act.
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Mayor/Do Governor intends to formulate, modify, or approve a master plan including matters under Article 11(1)12 or an implementation plan including matters falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (2), he or she shall consult with the heads of relevant administrative agencies in advance. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Jan. 30, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), Article 24 through 26 of the Framework Act on Administration shall apply mutatis mutandis to the standards, effects, etc. of the legal fiction under this Article. <Added on Jan. 30, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 9; previous Article 13 moved to Article 17 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 13-2 (Joint conference on legal fiction of authorization and permission) (1) In order to have a prior consultation on legal fiction of authorization, permission, etc. with the heads of relevant administrative agencies in accordance with Article 13(3), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor may hold a joint conference comprised of all relevant administrative agencies, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The head of a relevant administrative agency referred to in Article 13(3) shall request public officials under his or her jurisdiction to attend the joint conference held under paragraph (1). <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 17, 2012]
  [Moved from Article 9-2 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 14 (Access to land) (1) Where necessary to conduct an examination or land survey in connection with construction of the dam, the implementer of a dam construction project may enter or use land of a third party and may relocate or remove trees, rock, soil, or other obstacles therein, if deemed absolutely necessary.
  (2) When the implementer of a dam construction project intends to enter land of a third party pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the owner, occupant, or manager of the land of his or her intended date and place of entry no later than seven days before the intended date of entry; provided, if it is impossible to confirm the address or abode of the owner, occupant, or manager of the land, the implementer shall notify his or her intended date and place of entry to the head of the competent Si (in the case of a Special Self-Governing City, referring to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, and in the case of a Special Self-Governing Province, referring to the Special Self-Governing Province Governor; hereinafter the same shall apply), Gun, or Gu (hereinafter referred to as "head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu") <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012; Dec. 2, 2016>
  (3) Where the implementer of a dam construction project, other than an administrative authority, intends to enter land of a third party pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall obtain permission from the head of a competent Si/Gun/Gu. <Added on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (4) Where the implementer of a dam construction project intends to relocate or remove trees, rocks, soils or other obstacles pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall, in advance, obtain consent of the owner, occupant, or manager of such obstacles. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (5) If the implementer of a dam construction project is unable to obtain consent of the owner, occupant or manager of obstacles as required under paragraph (4) because it is impossible to confirm whereabouts of the owner, occupant or manager, he or she may relocate or remove such obstacles after giving notice thereof to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu; provided, if the implementer of a dam construction project is not an administrative authority, he or she shall obtain permission from the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (6) A person who intends to relocate or remove obstacles pursuant to paragraph (4) shall notify the owner, occupant or manager of such obstacles thereof no later than three days before the date when he or she intends to relocate or remove the obstacles; provided, this shall not apply in any case falling under paragraph (5). <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (7) No person shall enter a housing site or land of a third party enclosed by walls or fences without the consent of the occupant of the land before sunrise or after sunset. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (8) No occupant of land shall obstruct or disallow activities of the implementer of a dam construction project, prescribed in paragraph (1), without good cause. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (9) A person who intends to conduct any act under paragraph (1) shall carry an identification indicating his or her authority and present it to interested persons, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (10) If any person suffers a loss due to an activity referred to in paragraph (1), the implementer of the relevant dam construction project shall compensate for the loss. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (11) The implementer of a dam construction project and a person who suffers a loss due to the project shall try to reach an agreement on compensation for such loss under paragraph (10). <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (12) If the implementer of a dam construction project and a person who suffers a loss due to the project failed to, or were unable to, settle the compensation claims for such loss under paragraph (11), either party may file a petition for adjudication with the competent Land Expropriation Committee. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 10; previous Article 14 moved to Article 18 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 15 (Expropriation and use of land) (1) The implementer of a dam construction project may expropriate or use land, buildings and other fixtures on the land, rights and interests, except ownership, in the land, buildings, and fixtures, mining rights, fishing rights, aquaculture business rights, and the right to use water (hereinafter referred to as "land and other property"), as necessary for the construction of the dam. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
  (2) For purposes of applying paragraph (1), the public notice of a master plan under Article 11(4) shall be deemed project approval or the public notice of project approval under Article 20(1) or 22 of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects, while a petition for adjudication shall be filed within the project period specified in the relevant master plan, notwithstanding Article 23(1) and 28(1) of that Act. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  (3) The Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works shall apply mutatis mutandis to the expropriation and use of land and other property under paragraph (1), except as otherwise provided in this Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 11; previous Article 15 moved to Article 5 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 16 (Public notice of completion of dam construction) (1) When the construction of a dam is completed, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the competent Mayor/Do Governor shall, without delay, give public notice of the following matters: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The fact that the construction of the dam is completed;
  2. The river area, defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the River Act, in a dam construction area;
  3. The fact that the relevant dam is a river facility defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the River Act.
  (2) When the construction of a dam is completed, the implementer of the dam construction project, not the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor, shall, without delay, file an application for approval for completion with the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (or the competent Mayor/Do Governor, if the implementer of the dam construction project is the head of a Si/Gun; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article), as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall issue a certificate of approval for completion of the project and shall give public notice of the matters under paragraph (1), if the dam is deemed to have been built in accordance with the relevant implementation plan. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) When public notice is given pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), consent shall be deemed obtained pursuant to the proviso of the main clause of subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the River Act, and a river area shall also be deemed determined and publicly notified pursuant to Article 10(3) of that Act.
  (4) When public notice is given pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), inspection of the completion of the relevant work or project shall be deemed to have been conducted, authorization of the completion of the work or project obtained, and public notice given accordingly following authorization, permission, etc. of the relevant construction work or project based on the legal fiction under Article 13(2). <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 12; previous Article 16 moved to Article 6 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 16-2 (Use of dam before public notice of completion of construction of dam) (1) No implementer of a dam construction project shall use a dam unless he or she publicly notifies completion of the construction of the dam.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the implementer of a dam construction project may use the relevant dam even before he or she publicly notifies completion of the construction of the dam for immediate water supply, flood control, etc., as he or she may deem necessary; provided, this shall be limited to cases where an implementer of a dam construction project who is not the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Mayor/Do Governor obtains approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (where the head of a Si/Gun is the implementer of a dam construction project, referring to the Mayor/Do Governor). <Amended on Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2016]
  [Moved from Article 12-2 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 17 (Vesting of ownership of public facilities) (1) Where the implementer of a dam construction project installs a new public facility or a public facility that replaces an existing public facility, the ownership of the existing public facility shall be vested in the implementer of the dam construction project without consideration, while the ownership of the new public facility shall be vested in the State or the competent local government, whoever shall manage the facility, for no consideration, notwithstanding the State Property Act and the Public Property and Commodity Management Act.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to formulate, modify, or approve an implementation plan, which includes matters relating to the vesting of the ownership of a public facility under paragraph (1), he or she shall hear the opinions of the competent management agency responsible for management of the public facility; provided, if no management agency has yet to be designated, he or she shall hear the opinions of the competent management agency after such management agency is designated, but before the completion of the project is approved. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The implementer of a dam construction project shall notify the competent management agency of the types and detailed list of public facilities, the ownership of which shall be vested in the implementer, or public facilities, the ownership of which shall be vested in the State or a local government, before construction of the dam is completed, and the ownership of such public facilities shall be vested in the persons specified in paragraph (1) on the date when the completion of dam construction is publicly notified pursuant to Article 16. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  (4) For purposes of the registration of public facilities and land under paragraphs (1) through (3), the public notice of the relevant implementation plan under Article 12 and the public notice of the completion of the relevant dam construction under Article 16 shall be made in lieu of a written document verifying the cause of registration under the Registration of Real Estate Act. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  (5) The scope of public facilities referred to in paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 13; previous Article 17 moved to Article 7 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

Article 18 (Disposal of State or public property) (1) Any asset situated on a project site and owned by the State or a local government may be leased or transferred to the implementer of a dam construction project, notwithstanding the State Property Act or the Public Property and Commodity Management Act.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to formulate, modify, or approve an implementation plan, which involves the disposal of any State or public property under paragraph (1), he or she shall, in advance, consult with the management agency responsible for the management of the property. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) If it is unclear which administrative agency is responsible for the State property to be leased or transferred to the implementer of a dam construction project in accordance with paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall be deemed a competent management agency. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 14; previous Article 18 moved to Article 8 <Jun. 15, 2021>]

SECTION 2 Special Cases concerning Construction and Management of Multipurpose Dams

Article 19 (Grantees of dam usage rights) (1) In order to be granted the right to use a dam, a person shall file an application for the grant of the right to use the dam and shall satisfy the prerequisites specified in Article 25.
  (2) An heir, a corporation incorporated in the course of a merger, or any other person who universally succeeds to a grantee of dam usage rights shall succeed to the status of the grantee of dam usage rights.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 20 (Bearing of costs of construction of multipurpose dams) (1) A grantee of dam usage rights shall contribute an amount apportioned to him or her out of the cost of construction of the relevant multipurpose dam, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The following costs and expenses shall be taken into consideration in apportioning the cost to be contributed by a grantee of dam usage rights under paragraph (1):
  1. The estimate of investment calculated on the basis of utility obtained by using the water stored in the relevant multipurpose dam for the use for which the right to use the dam is to be granted;
  2. The estimate of the cost necessary for the installation of a structure to be exclusively used for the purpose of use for which the right to use the dam is to be granted to obtain utility, up to the same level as the utility that the structure can provide.
  (3) An amount calculated by subtracting the portion to be borne under paragraph (1) from the construction cost of the relevant multipurpose dam shall be borne by the National Treasury.
  (4) If a grantee of dam usage rights fails to obtain the right to use the dam, the State shall refund the amount contributed by the person as a portion of cost pursuant to paragraph (1) to the person; provided, the refunding of such amount may be suspended until, if any, a third grantee of dam usage rights is designated, except where the relevant plan is abolished.
  (5) Where the return of an amount contributed as a portion of cost pursuant to the proviso of paragraph (4), the State shall pay interest for the period of suspension, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (6) Matters necessary for the scope of cost necessary for the construction of a multipurpose dam, the payment method for the portion of cost to be contributed, the payment deadline for such contribution, and the refund of such contribution shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 21 (Prior investment by State) If it is necessary to control floods or to fulfill other special purposes, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may build a multipurpose dam without designating a grantee of dam usage rights. In such case, the provisions governing a grantee of dam usage rights or a dam usage right-holder shall become applicable at the time the grantee of dam usage rights or the dam usage right-holder is designated. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 22 (Expenses borne by local governments) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may require the local governments that substantially benefit from the construction of a multipurpose dam (excluding the local government having jurisdiction over the area in which the dam is to be built) to bear the expenses that otherwise shall be borne by the National Treasury in accordance with Article 20 (3) out of the expenses necessary for the construction of the dam, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 23 Deleted. <Mar. 25, 2025>

Article 23-2 Deleted. <Mar. 25, 2025>

Article 23-3 Deleted. <Mar. 25, 2025>

Article 24 (Granting of right to use dam) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall grant the right to use a multipurpose dam to a person who intends to use the water stored in the dam for a specific use, upon receipt of an application therefrom. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment grants the right to use a dam in accordance with paragraph (1), he or she shall clarify the following: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Objectives of the granting;
  2. The highest and lowest levels of water to be secured in accordance with the right to use the relevant dam granted, and the water storage capacity.
  (3) The matters under paragraph (2)2 shall be determined in a way that maximizes the utility of the relevant dam.
  (4) When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment gives public notice of the completion of dam construction in accordance with Article 16, he or she shall, without delay, grant the right to use the dam to the grantee of dam usage rights. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) A grantee of dam usage rights is to be granted may use the water stored in the dam for a specific purpose with permission from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, even before the right to use the dam is granted to that person in accordance with paragraph (1). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 25 (Prerequisites for granting of right to use dam) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall grant the right to use a dam to an applicant, only if the applicant satisfies the following prerequisites: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. If the purpose of using the water stored in the relevant multipurpose dam conforms to the purpose of obtaining the right to use the dam;
  2. If the applicant has obtained permission or authorization necessary to use the water stored in the multipurpose dam.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 26 (Restrictions on occupancy and use of river for specific purposes) A person who intends to use water stored in a multipurpose dam for a specific purpose shall obtain a permit to occupy and use the river under Article 33 of the River Act and a permit to use water from the river under Article 50 of that Act and then shall obtain the right to use the dam under Article 24.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 27 (Refusal to grant right to use dam) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may refuse to grant the right to use a dam to a person not designated in the relevant master plan as a grantee of dam usage rights. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall refuse to grant the right to use a dam to the grantee of dam usage rights in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. If the grantee of dam usage rights fails to satisfy the prerequisites specified in Article 25;
  2. If the grantee of dam usage rights fails to pay his or her contribution prescribed in Article 20(1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 28 (Areas to be covered by stored water) The area for which stored water may be secured in accordance with the right to use a dam shall be limited to the area demarcated by a line along which the highest level of stored water, as defined in Article 24(2)2, abuts onto the land.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 29 (Nature of right to use dam) The right to use a dam shall be deemed a real right, and the provisions regarding real property shall apply mutatis mutandis to the right to use a dam, except as otherwise provided for in this Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 30 (Restrictions on disposal of right to use dam) (1) A right to use a dam shall not be the subject matter of any other right, except where such right becomes the subject matter of inheritance, a merger of, or other universal succession to, a corporation, a transfer, a disposition of tax in arrears, compulsory execution, or a mortgage.
  (2) If a right to use a dam becomes subject to a registered mortgage, such right shall not be divided, merged, or waived, nor shall the purpose of the grant of the right be changed, unless the mortgagee consents thereto.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 31 (Revocation of right to use dam) (1) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment cannot provide the same access to water in a river to any person other than the dam usage right-holder as before, as he or she intends to fully or partially revoke the permission to occupy and use the river under Article 33 of the River Act and the permission to use river water under Article 50 of that Act, which have been granted to the dam usage right-holder, he or she shall revoke or modify the right to use the dam, along with revocation of the permission, etc. to occupy and use the dam granted to the dam usage right-holder. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) If it is necessary to provide access to use water river to any person, in addition to the dam usage right-holder, as the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to fully or partially revoke the permission to occupy and use a river under Article 33 of the River Act and the permission to occupy and use water in the river under Article 50 of that Act, which have been granted to the dam usage right-holder, he or she may order the dam usage right-holder to fully or partially transfer the right to use the dam to a third party within a reasonable period of time. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) If the dam usage right-holder does not transfer his or her right to use the dam within the period under paragraph (2), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may fully or partially revoke the right to use the dam, only where any person other than the dam usage right-holder is eligible to obtain permission to use river water on the same terms and conditions as those of the permission granted to the dam usage right-holder for the use of river water. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) If it is impossible to maintain the right to use a dam in any of the following cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may fully or partially revoke or modify the right to use the dam. In such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall hear the opinions of the dam usage right-holder: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. If the water storage capacity has been changed as a result of the extension or reconstruction of the dam;
  2. If the pattern of the use of water stored in the dam has changed.
  (5) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment revokes or modifies the right to use a dam in accordance with paragraph (4), he or she shall amend and publicly notify the provisions regarding the distribution of stored water by purpose in the relevant master plan publicly notified in accordance with Article 11(4). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 32 (Registration) (1) The right to use a dam, the creation, alteration, transfer, or dissolution of a mortgage relating thereto, or a restriction on the disposal of the right to use a dam shall be ineffective, unless entered in the register of rights to use a dam, which shall be kept in the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Except as provided in paragraph (1), matters necessary for the registration of rights to use a dam and mortgages relating thereto shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 33 (Charges) Except where the right to use a dam has been granted under Article 24(4), a dam usage right-holder shall pay charges in an amount calculated using the formula prescribed by Presidential Decree to the National Treasury. The estimated amount of investment calculated in accordance with Article 20(2)1 shall be taken into consideration in calculating charges in such cases.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 34 (Refunds of contributions) (1) When the State revokes or alters a right to use a dam under Article 31, it shall partially refund the contributions paid pursuant to Article 20(1) or charges paid pursuant to Article 33.
  (2) The amount to be refunded pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be calculated in the same way as the relevant contributions or charges are calculated, but the amount to be refunded to a grantee of dam usage rights pursuant to Article 24(4) shall not exceed an amount calculated by subtracting the depreciation cost calculated by the formula prescribed by Presidential Decree from the contributions paid pursuant to Article 20(1).
  (3) If the creation of a mortgage is registered over a right to use a dam, which is extinguished by revocation or alteration under Article 31, the State shall deposit the amount refundable under paragraph (1) in the competent court, except where the mortgagee consents to such revocation or alteration. In such cases, the mortgagee over the right to use a dam may exercise his or her rights on the refund so deposited.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 35 (Collection of usage charges) (1) A dam usage right-holder or a grantee of dam usage rights may collect usage charges from persons who use water stored in the dam; provided, no usage charge shall be collected from a person who uses water from a river with the permission to use water from the river under Article 50 of the River Act.
  (2) A dam usage right-holder or a grantee of dam usage rights shall take into consideration the volume of water used by a person who uses water stored in the dam in determining the amount of usage charges under paragraph (1), which shall not exceed the amount that he or she has contributed pursuant to Article 20(1) or the amount he or she has paid pursuant to Article 33.
  (3) When a dam usage right-holder or a grantee of dam usage rights intends to collect usage charges in accordance with paragraph (1), he or she shall determine the methods for calculating and collecting such usage charges and the deadline for payment, etc. and shall, in advance, obtain the approval of the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 36 (Apportionment of expenses to manage multipurpose dams) (1) Expenses incurred in maintaining, repairing, or managing a multipurpose dam shall be borne by the National Treasury, the dam usage right-holder or the grantee of dam usage rights. No expenses to be borne by the manager entrusted with the management of the dam shall exceed his or her revenue from the entrusted management of the dam.
  (2) Matters necessary for the allocation of expenses under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 37 (Compulsory collection of contributions) (1) Where any person fails to pay contributions or charges falling under any of the following, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall send a demand letter to the person urging him or her to pay the amount by the payment deadline determined by the Minister: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 8, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Contributions under Article 20(1);
  2. Deleted; <Mar. 25, 2025>
  3. Charges under Article 33;
  4. Contributions by a dam usage right-holder under Article 36.
  (2) Where a person who received such demand under paragraph (1) fails to pay contributions or charges by the deadline for payment, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may collect such contributions or charges in the same manner as delinquent national taxes. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 2, 2016; Jun. 8, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The right to collect contributions or charges under paragraph (1) shall be extinguished by prescription unless the right is exercised for 5 years. <Amended on Dec. 2, 2016; Mar. 25, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 38 (Recognition of multipurpose dams) (1) If it is found that a dam built by any person, other than the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, has great utility as a multipurpose dam, he or she may pay the amount of compensation deemed reasonable to a person who built the dam, through negotiations with the person, and may designate the dam as a multipurpose dam under this Act. In such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may grant the right to use the dam to a person who built the dam, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment designates a non-multipurpose dam as a multipurpose dam in accordance with paragraph (1), he or she shall publicly notify the following matters without delay, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Grounds for recognizing the dam as a multipurpose dam;
  2. Matters prescribed in Article 11(1)2 through 4;
  3. Matters regarding compensation to the person who built the dam.
  (3) If it is found that a dam under construction by any person, other than the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, has great utility as a multipurpose dam, the State may partially subsidize the costs of constructing the dam or may arrange a loan for the construction. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) When the State provides a subsidy or arranges a loan in accordance with paragraph (3), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may require the recipient of such subsidy or loan to provide instructions on the construction of the dam or may require such person to inspect the dam facilities under construction or to submit a report thereon. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

CHAPTER III SUPPORT FOR LOCAL RESIDENTS IN DAM CONSTRUCTION AREA

Article 39 (Support for persons not relocated to designated settlement) The implementer of a dam construction project may provide support to persons who do not move to the designated settlement, among residents relocated from the area to be submerged, and other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, taking into consideration the loss of their hometown and basis of their livelihood due to the construction of the dam. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 40 (Support for persons relocated from area to be submerged) (1) If it is necessary to build houses for residents relocated from an area to be submerged and develop a basis of their livelihood, thus smoothing out the process of relocation and settlement, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to give priority to helping them by tapping into the Housing and Urban Fund under the Housing and Urban Fund Act. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) If it is necessary to develop a basis of the livelihood of the residents relocated from an area to be submerged, the implementer of a relevant dam construction project may request the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment to give priority to supporting them through the Housing and Urban Fund under the Housing and Urban Fund Act. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The head of a local government having jurisdiction over a dam construction area or an area to which water will be supplied from the dam to be built and the dam custodian may provide vocational training programs to residents relocated from the area to be submerged and may take other measures necessary to provide livelihood support to those residents, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 41 (Projects on improvement of dam environs) (1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over areas adjacent to the dam that meets or exceeds the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree shall implement projects on the improvement of areas adjacent to dams (hereinafter referred to as "projects on the improvement of dam environs") during the period of dam construction in order to promote economic growth in the dam environs and improve the living environment, taking into consideration changes likely to occur due to the construction of the dam.
  (2) Where a master plan for constructing a dam of a size that meets the standard under paragraph (1) is publicly notified, the Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction over the area in which the main clause of the dam will be built shall, without delay, formulate a plan pertaining to projects on the improvement of dam environs and shall obtain the approval of the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment; provided, the plan does not require the approval of the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, if the dam is built by a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor intends to formulate a plan relating to the improvement of dam environs under paragraph (2), he or she shall have a prior consultation with the implementer of a relevant dam construction project and the head of a competent Si/Gun/Gu.
  (4) Further details regarding the scope of dam environs under paragraph (1) and projects on the improvement of dam environs shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 42 (Financial resources for projects on improvement of dam environs) (1) No cost of projects on the improvement of dam environs shall exceed the standard amount calculated by adding a factor determined by the method prescribed by Presidential Decree, taking into consideration the water storage area, gross water storage capacity, and demand for development based on households from the submerged area, and local requirements, to the basic amount based on either of the following gross water storage capacity: <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  1. At least 20 million cubic meters but less than 150 million cubic meters: 30 billion won;
  2. At least 150 million cubic meters: 40 billion won.
  (2) The cost of the projects on the improvement of dam environs shall be borne by the implementer of the relevant dam construction project and the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu.
  (3) Specific guidelines for allocating the cost of projects on the improvement of dam environs under paragraph (2) and matters regarding the management of such projects shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 43 (Projects for supporting dam environs) (1) In order to boost the income of residents in the dam environs specified by Presidential Decree and to enhance the welfare of such residents, the competent dam management agency or the dam custodian shall formulate a project plan for supporting dam environs (hereinafter referred to as "projects for supporting dam environs") for each year after the construction of the dam is completed, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where a dam management agency or the dam custodian intends to formulate a project plan for supporting dam environs pursuant to paragraph (1), the dam management agency or the dam custodian shall consult, in advance, with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the dam environs.
  (3) When the dam custodian formulates a project plan for supporting dam environs pursuant to paragraph (1), the person shall submit such plan to the competent dam management agency.
  (4) A project for supporting dam environs shall be implemented by any of the following persons, as prescribed by Presidential Decree:
  1. The competent dam management agency or dam custodian;
  2. The head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the relevant dam environs.
  (5) The extent of an area for which a project plan for supporting dam environs shall be formulated shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 44 (Financial resources for projects for supporting dam environs) (1) Funds necessary for projects for supporting dam environs shall be procured from the following sources; provided, contributions under subparagraphs 1 and 2 shall be provided only for a dam eligible for the projects for supporting dam environs under Article 43:
  1. Contribution by the competent dam management agency or the dam usage right-holder;
  2. Contribution by waterworks business entities supplying tap water or industrial water from the dam;
  3. Borrowings;
  4. Revenues accruing from the management of the funds procured.
  (2) The competent dam management agency, the dam usage right-holder, or the waterworks business entities supplying tap water or industrial water from the dam shall pay an amount at either of the following rates as a contribution referred to in paragraph (1)1 and 2, respectively: <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  1. Not more than 6/100 of revenues from the sale of power during the year immediately before the previous year;
  2. Not more than 22/100 of the amount calculated by multiplying the volume of tap and industrial water sold during the year immediately before the previous year by a unit price of dam water set by the Korea Water Resources Corporation for the year immediately before the previous year.
  (3) The funds procured pursuant to paragraph (1) for projects for supporting dam environs shall be managed by the competent dam management agency or the dam custodian. In such cases, the competent dam management agency or the dam custodian shall manage the funds for projects for supporting dam environs under paragraph (1) in a separate account.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 44-2 (Creation of environment-friendly spaces in dam environs) (1) In order to promote the development of dam environs, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over dam environs under Article 41 and 43 may implement projects for creating space for recreational, cultural, and leisure activities (hereinafter referred to as "environment-friendly space"), including nature-learning places, ecology parks, and water sports facilities utilizing lakes, swamps, and surrounding scenery, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to the extent that such projects do not adversely affect the total water pollution load control system under the Water Environment Conservation Act and other relevant Acts. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2017>
  (2) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to implement a project for creating environment-friendly space in dam environs pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall have a prior consultation with the competent dam management agency.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 44-3 (Preferential establishment of public facilities) The head of a central administrative agency or the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu may prioritize constructing roads, bridges, waterworks, and other public facilities or implement other projects specified by Presidential Decree in dam environs in order to promote the economy in the dam environs and to improve habitats in the dam environs.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 44-4 (Installation of public sewerage) (1) If necessary to perform an agreement made pursuant to the provisions of Article 27 through 31 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act (including re-consultation under Article 21 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act and plans for environmental conservation under Article 33 of that Act), the implementer of a dam construction project may be entrusted with installation of a public sewerage system from the competent public sewerage management authority under Article 74(3) of the Sewerage Act and may install the public sewerage system during the construction period of the dam. <Amended on Jul. 21, 2011>
  (2) The apportionment to a local government, of the cost of installing a public sewerage system under paragraph (1), may be reapportioned to either of the following persons:
  1. A grantee of dam usage rights who is to use water stored in the dam for tap water and industrial water, if the dam is a multipurpose dam;
  2. A waterworks business entity, if the dam is a dam for tap water or industrial water.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

CHAPTER IV SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 45 (Authority of Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment) (1) Where a master plan for constructing a dam is publicly notified, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall make the following dispositions, etc. for the project site and the river area publicly notified in accordance with Article 16(1) and (2), notwithstanding the River Act and the Aggregate Extraction Act; provided, if the dam is to be built by a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun, the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of the Si/Gun shall make the following dispositions, etc.; if the management of the dam is entrusted in accordance with Article 5(2), such dispositions, etc. shall be made by the dam custodian, within the scope of entrustment, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Jun. 15, 2021; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Permission to implement river works under Article 30 of the River Act, and permission to occupy and use a river under Article 33 of that Act, and the permission to use water from a river under Article 50 of that Act;
  2. Acceptance of a report on the devolution to rights and obligations under Article 5(2) of the River Act from a person to whom the permission under subparagraph 1 has been granted;
  3. Designation of an area in which camping and cooking, and fishing, which pollutes rivers with bait, such as past bait and fish meal, under subparagraph 6 of Article 46 of the River Act are prohibited;
  4. Permission to extract aggregate under Article 22 of the Aggregate Extraction Act;
  5. The following dispositions made in relation to dispositions, etc. prescribed in subparagraphs 1 through 4:
  a. The revocation of a permission or the suspension of construction works under Article 69 or 70 of the River Act;
  b. The revocation of permission to extract aggregate or suspension of the extraction of aggregate under Article 31 of the Aggregate Extraction Act; and reinstatement order under Article 33 of that Act.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, a Mayor/Do Governor, the head of a Si/Gun, or a dam custodian intends to make any disposition under paragraph (1), he or she shall, in advance, consult with the heads of the relevant administrative agencies. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun intends to make any disposition under paragraph (1), he or she shall, in advance, hear the opinions of the relevant dam custodian. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) A dam custodian may collect rents or usage charges from a person to whom permission has been granted under paragraph (1)1 or 4 and may use such rents or usage charges to cover expenses incurred in managing the dam, notwithstanding the provisions of Article 37(1) through (4) of the River Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]
  [Title Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025]

Article 46 (Relationship to River Act) Except as otherwise provided for in this Act, the River Act shall apply to necessary matters regarding the construction and management of dams.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 47 (Hearings) When a dam management agency intends to make either of the following dispositions, it shall hold a hearing:
  1. An order to transfer a right to use the relevant dam under Article 31(2);
  2. The revocation of permission under Article 45(1)5.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 48 (Delegation of authority) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may partially delegate his or her authority under this Act to the heads of affiliated agencies or a Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun may entrust part of his or her business, such as conducting investigations and research, or constructing a dam under this Act, to a corporation, organization or agency related to dams or cause them to act on his or her behalf. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 48-2 (Subsidized construction of dam) The State or a Mayor/Do Governor may bear all or some of the expenses incurred by a local government in executing a dam construction project as the implementer for the cost incurred for the dam construction project, within budgetary limits.
  [This Article Added on May 30, 2011]

CHAPTER V PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 49 (Penalty provisions) Any executive officer or employee of a dam custodian who causes damage to the public or hinders flood control in violation of Article 8 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than five years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 50 Deleted. <Dec. 7, 2005>

Article 51 (Penalty provisions) Any person, other than an administrative authority, who engages in an activity specified in Article 14(1) without permission or consent required under Article 14(3) through (5) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than six months or by a fine not exceeding three million won. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012; Jun. 15, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

Article 52 (Joint penalty provisions) Where the representative of a corporation, or an agent, servant or any other employee of a corporation or individual commits an offence under Article 49 or 51 with respect to the business of such corporation or individual, not only shall the offender be punished but the corporation or individual also shall be punished by a fine under the relevant provisions; provided, this shall not apply to cases where such corporation or individual has not been neglected to pay due attention and supervision concerning the business in order to prevent such violation.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 1, 2009]

Article 53 (Administrative fines) (1) Any person who obstructs or refuses an act conducted pursuant to Article 14(1) without good cause shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding three million won. <Amended on Jun. 15, 2021>
  (2) Administrative fines under paragraph (1) shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the competent Mayor/Do Governor (limited to administrative fines imposed for offenses committed in connection with a dam built by the Mayor/Do Governor), as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 8, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 30, 2011]

